<h1>Orcraft Result Fields</h1>
<p>A result reports whatever measurements its component chose to declare, on top of the status and
  descriptions every result carries. This page covers the declared fields, the inherited ones, and the
  counters each level sums from the level below. How the status itself is set is covered in
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/statuses/">Orcraft Statuses</a>.</p>

<h2 id="result-fields">Result fields</h2>
<p>Result fields come from the <code>OrcraftResultField</code> namespace and are declared on a result
  class, exactly the way config fields are declared on a config class:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('class MyTestResult(OrcraftTestResult):
	measured_value = OrcraftResultField.Numeric(default=0, label="Measured Value")
	within_limits = OrcraftResultField.Boolean(default=False, label="Within Limits")
	output_file = OrcraftResultField.Text(default="", label="Output File")
	log_orientation = OrcraftResultField.Format(
		default=OrcraftResultField.LogTableOrientation.VERTICAL
	)') }}

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Value</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Text</code></td>
      <td><code>str</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Numeric</code></td>
      <td><code>int</code> or <code>float</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Boolean</code></td>
      <td><code>bool</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Format</code></td>
      <td>Presentation-only, used for <code>log_orientation</code>.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Each accepts <code>default</code>, <code>default_factory</code>, <code>label</code> and
  <code>formatter</code>, with the same meanings they have for
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/#options">config fields</a>. A field with
  a <code>label</code> appears in the logged result table and in the controller; one without stays
  internal.</p>

<p>A <code>formatter</code> changes how a value is displayed without changing what is stored, which is
  how a raw number becomes a percentage or a status becomes a colored word in the log.</p>

<h2 id="inherited-fields">Fields every result has</h2>
<p>Result classes inherit a set of fields the framework maintains itself. You read them, and in a few
  cases write them, but you never declare them.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>status</code></td>
      <td>The component's outcome - one of the values listed under <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/statuses/#statuses">Statuses</a>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-fail">failure_description</code></td>
      <td>Why the system under test was judged incorrect. Setting it moves a passing result to <code class="status-fail">FAIL</code> on its own.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-error">error_description</code></td>
      <td>Why the component could not complete.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-warning">warning_description</code></td>
      <td>Something worth flagging that did not change the status.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>finish_time</code></td>
      <td>When the component finished.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>elapsed_time</code></td>
      <td>How long it took.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_orientation</code></td>
      <td>Whether the result table is logged horizontally or vertically.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="aggregation">Aggregated counters</h2>
<p>Each level above a case counts what happened beneath it, so a summary is available without any code
  keeping score:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Result</th>
      <th>Counters</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>OrcraftTestResult</code></td>
      <td><code>total_cases</code>, <code>passed_cases</code>, <code>failed_cases</code>, <code>error_cases</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_cases</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>OrcraftTestGroupResult</code></td>
      <td>The same case counters, plus <code>total_tests</code>, <code>passed_tests</code>, <code>failed_tests</code>, <code>error_tests</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_tests</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>OrcraftTestSuiteResult</code></td>
      <td>The case and test counters, plus <code>total_groups</code>, <code>passed_groups</code>, <code>failed_groups</code>, <code>error_groups</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_groups</code></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Because case counts carry all the way to the top, a suite result answers both questions at once: how
  many of its groups came back clean, and how many individual cases were actually executed to get
  there.</p>
